  "final_span": "on the other side of the Cape, to the south. He is perceived highly in terms of likability but with equal levels of distrust from both sides.\nOn the second wind-swept portion of Cape, n\u00e4wahnit Martin (pronounced \"nawnahin\") campaigned for a runaway immigration program without a chance of passage and also vowed to challenge the Massachusetts Health Care Reform Law, as per the Salem News's editorial policy, which backed her candidacy due to her identity, as noted in the editorial column. The small-town newspapers in the district tend to support pro-government solutions while opposing taxes.\nfor Norfolk County District Attorney.\nIt serves no purpose to criticise Norfolk County District Attorney William Keating for his lack of comprehension regarding the crucial vehicle for economic reform. Virtually speaking, it is a scientific truth that increasing airline service in a city does not guarantee thousands of new jobs.\nFor incumbent Republican David D'Arcangelo, who defeated Keating for his seat in 2008, the level of laughter would be three times greater at an annual meeting of airline pilots. It could be greater if run directly by the airlines, yet there is no use in wishing publicly for additional flights or service enhancements unless the starting point is near the city boundaries. Similarly, this applies to the business world. The job is here. Examine the employment prospects in the informational economy.\nSalem News presents a query with two options: what action should we take next in the North Shore? I'll give a mixed answer. Move ahead, pause, perhaps go backwards. I do not know the potential future, ahead. Right now, I prefer something more pleasant.\nThe combined result would hinge on one's belief about the prevalence of the economic and political ideology; truly, there's no point in a second glance, as the matter is concluded, spent, kaput. In that case taking Reagan's line \"We are a nation of skeptics. Few of us have any interest in authority, expertise or dogma. We are, to a degree, anti-institutional, and this distrust of settled ways, this desire to upset and overturn, is preserved, not eroded, in the Internet age\" might be best.\nActually I suspect that there is plenty of Filmatism especially in",
